course: dictionary
lesson: 3183
title: One word, two meanings: enclosure
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning the writer means by reading the words around it. The first meaning of enclosure is an area with a fence or wall all round it, often to keep animals in. «Keepers sometimes spray perfume or spices in an enclosure» tells you the animals live inside a fenced space.
explain: The second meaning is something put inside an envelope with a letter. «Gran's letter had a photo as an enclosure» shows this, because a photo can be posted in an envelope with a letter. Ask yourself: is this a place, or a thing put inside something else? That question helps you choose the right meaning.
explain: Look at the sentence and the clues. «Good zoos change the enclosure and give puzzles» talks about changing a place for animals, so it is the fenced area. Swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: An enclosure is an area with a fence or wall all round it.
fact: Zoos, farms and wildlife parks often keep animals in enclosures.
fact: An enclosure can also be something put inside an envelope with a letter.
fact: «Pacing» means walking the same line again and again, and it can mean an animal is bored.
fact: The clues around a word, such as «inside the enclosure» or «the lion enclosure», tell you which meaning fits.
exercise: «Keepers sometimes spray perfume or spices in an enclosure, because new smells give cats something to explore.» What is an enclosure here? | A kind of perfume bottle | *An area with a fence or wall all round it | A bag of spices for cooking | A card that goes inside an envelope
exercise: Gran's letter came with a photo as an enclosure. What does enclosure mean here? | A room for the dog | A place to wait for the post | A fence round the garden | *Something put inside the envelope with the letter
exercise: «At the zoo, follow the signs, stay behind the barriers and never climb fences, because zoo animals are wild and can be dangerous.» Which word tells you zoo animals may be unsafe? | barriers | *dangerous | fences | signs
test: «Walking the same line again and again is called pacing; it can mean a cat is bored.» What is pacing? | Eating slowly from a bowl | Sleeping all day in the sun | *Walking the same line again and again | Jumping very high onto a shelf
test: «Many animals have quiet off-show areas where they can rest and hide; good zoos let animals choose whether to be seen, which is why you sometimes see an empty enclosure.» Why might an enclosure look empty? | The zoo has closed for ever | The enclosure is made of glass | Keepers forgot to feed the animals | *The animal chose to rest out of sight
exam: Ben draws a square fenced space for the goats in his maths book, then posts a letter with a drawing tucked inside. Which word fits the fenced space? | *enclosure | envelope | barrier | puzzle
